.TH MESHCORE\-TUI 1 "2026" "meshcore-tui" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
meshcore\-tui \- terminal UI companion client for MeshCore mesh radio nodes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B meshcore\-tui
[\fB\-\-transport\fR \fItype\fR]
[\fB\-\-device\fR \fIaddress\fR]
[\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB\-\-debug\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmeshcore\-tui\fR is a terminal UI companion client that connects a laptop
to a MeshCore mesh radio node over Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE), serial, or TCP.
It provides chat, channels, node discovery, and device configuration in a
five-tab interface.
.PP
If no \fB\-\-device\fR is given and the transport is BLE, an interactive
scan picker launches so you can choose your node.
.PP
Contact names in the Chat tab are prefixed with a colour-coded freshness dot
(\fB\(bu\fR): green = seen within 5 minutes, yellow = within 1 hour, red =
older or never seen.
Delivery-confirmed outbound messages display the mesh round-trip time
alongside the check mark (e.g.\& \fB\(ck 42ms\fR).
.PP
New messages that arrive while a different tab is active trigger a desktop
notification via \fBnotify-send\fR(1) if it is installed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-transport " " \fItype\fR
Transport to use.
Valid values: \fBble\fR (default), \fBserial\fR, \fBtcp\fR.
Overrides \fIdefault_transport\fR from the config file.
.TP
.BR \-\-device " " \fIaddress\fR
Device to connect to directly, skipping the BLE scan picker.
BLE: MAC address (e.g.\& \fBAA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\fR).
Serial: port path (e.g.\& \fB/dev/ttyUSB0\fR).
TCP: host:port (e.g.\& \fB192.168.1.100:3000\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " " \fIname\fR
Load connection settings from a named profile in the config file.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log BLE operations and protocol messages to stderr.
Useful for diagnosing connection problems.
Redirect with \fB2>/tmp/mc.log\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version string and exit.
.SH "KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS"
.SS Global
.TP
.B 1\-5
Switch directly to a tab: Chat (1), Channels (2), Nodes (3), Device (4), Settings (5).
These keys are suppressed while a text input is focused; press \fBEsc\fR to blur
the input first if needed.
.TP
.B Alt+N \fR/\fB Alt+P
Next / previous tab (configurable; default \fIalt+n\fR / \fIalt+p\fR).
Tab and Shift+Tab are passed through to the active tab for field navigation.
.TP
.B q \fR/\fB Ctrl+C
Quit.
\fBq\fR is suppressed while a text input is focused; \fBCtrl+C\fR always quits.
.SS "BLE scan picker (startup)"
.TP
.B \(ua \fR/\fB \(da \fR, \fB j \fR/\fB k
Navigate the device list.
.TP
.B Enter
Connect to the selected device.
.TP
.B r
Clear the list and restart the scan.
.TP
.B q \fR/\fB Ctrl+C
Cancel and exit.
.SS "Chat tab"
Outbound direct messages show a delivery status indicator after the text:
.IP "\fB\&...\fR" 4
BLE write in progress.
.IP "\fB\(bu sent\fR" 4
Device accepted the message and has a known path to the recipient.
.IP "\fB\(bu sent (no path)\fR" 4
Device sent as a blind flood; no cached route to recipient.
.IP "\fB\(ck 42ms\fR" 4
Remote node confirmed receipt; round-trip time shown.
.IP "\fB? no ack\fR" 4
No remote confirmation after 30 seconds.
Press \fBr\fR (with input blurred) to retry, up to 3 times.
.PP
.TP
.B \(ua \fR/\fB \(da
Move between contacts (or navigate messages in select mode).
.TP
.B /
Open contact search filter.
.TP
.B Escape
Clear search, exit select mode, or blur the input.
.TP
.B PgUp \fR/\fB Ctrl+U\fR, \fBPgDn \fR/\fB Ctrl+D
Scroll message history.
.TP
.BI "Type text" ", then " Enter
Compose and send a direct message.
.TP
.B /ping
Ping the selected contact.
Reply appears inline as \fI\(em ping reply: 234ms \(em\fR.
.TP
.B /trace
Trace the mesh path to the selected contact.
Reply appears inline as \fI\(em trace: 2 hops out, 1 hops back \(em\fR.
.TP
.BI "/location " "lat lon"
Set your advertised GPS coordinates on the device (decimal degrees,
e.g.\& \fB/location 4.099645 \-7.403468\fR).
Coordinates you supply are pushed to the firmware; the SDK does not read
from the hardware GPS sensor.
.PP
Typing \fB/\fR only opens contact search when the input is empty.
Starting \fB/ping\fR, \fB/trace\fR, or \fB/location\fR does not trigger search.
.TP
.B r
Retry the last unacknowledged direct message (input must be blurred; press
\fBEscape\fR first if needed).
Up to 3 retry attempts per message.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Broadcast a self-advertisement to the mesh.
A system message \fI\(em advert sent \(em\fR appears inline on completion.
.TP
.B s
Toggle select mode.
The input box turns blue and shows navigation hints.
Use \fB\(ua\fR/\fB\(da\fR to move the \fB\(rsaquo\fR cursor,
\fBd\fR to delete the highlighted message from the screen and local storage,
and \fBs\fR or \fBEscape\fR to exit.
.TP
.B X
Clear all messages for the selected contact.
The first press shows an inline confirmation; the second press executes.
Affects local storage only.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Toggle off-the-record mode.
While active the input border turns red, new messages are displayed in-session
but not written to local storage, and are lost when the app exits.
Press \fBCtrl+O\fR again to resume normal persistence.
.SS "Channels tab"
.TP
.B \(ua \fR/\fB \(da
Move between channels (or navigate messages in select mode).
.TP
.B PgUp \fR/\fB Ctrl+U\fR, \fBPgDn \fR/\fB Ctrl+D
Scroll message history.
.TP
.BI "Type text" ", then " Enter
Compose and send a channel message.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Broadcast a self-advertisement to the mesh.
.TP
.B n
Enter join mode.
Type a channel name and press Enter.
Optionally enter a PSK and press Enter again (leave blank to auto-derive the
secret from the channel name via SHA-256).
Typing \fBPublic\fR as the name uses the well-known shared secret automatically.
The input border turns yellow while in join mode.
.TP
.B d \fR(twice, outside select mode)
Leave the selected channel.
The first press shows an inline confirmation prompt; the second press executes.
The slot is cleared on the device; local message history is preserved.
.TP
.B Escape
Cancel join mode, exit select mode, or return to normal input.
.TP
.B s
Toggle select mode (same behaviour as in the Chat tab).
Note: \fBd\fR in select mode deletes a message; outside select mode it
initiates the leave-channel confirmation.
.TP
.B X
Clear all messages for the selected channel (local only, two-press confirm).
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Toggle off-the-record mode (same behaviour as in the Chat tab).
.SS "Nodes tab"
Discovered nodes are listed with Name, Type (chat/repeat/room/sensor),
SNR, RSSI, and Last Seen.
The list is session-only and resets on reconnect.
.PP
Selecting a node shows a detail bar beneath the list containing:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhops\fR \(em number of mesh hops to reach the node
.IP \(bu 2
\fBloc\fR \(em advertised GPS coordinates (lat,lon) if set by the node
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlast_advert\fR \(em timestamp from the node's own firmware
.IP \(bu 2
\fBping\fR and \fBtrace\fR results when measured
.TP
.B \(ua \fR/\fB \(da \fR, \fB k \fR/\fB j
Move selection up / down.
.TP
.B p
Ping the selected node (\fBSendStatusReq\fR).
Round-trip time appears in the detail bar when the reply arrives.
.TP
.B t
Trace the mesh path to the selected node (\fBSendPathDiscoveryReq\fR).
Hop counts appear in the detail bar when the reply arrives.
.SH "KEY BINDING CONFIGURATION"
Any key binding can be overridden in the \fB[keys]\fR section of
\fI~/.config/meshcore/config.toml\fR.
Key strings use BubbleTea notation: \fBalt+n\fR, \fBctrl+a\fR, \fBenter\fR, \fBpgup\fR, etc.
Omit a key to keep its default.
.PP
Available binding names and defaults:
.RS
.nf
next_tab      = "alt+n"
prev_tab      = "alt+p"
quit          = "q"
send          = "enter"
scroll_up     = "pgup"
scroll_down   = "pgdn"
advert        = "ctrl+a"
search        = "/"
select_mode   = "s"
delete_msg    = "d"
clear_all     = "X"
off_record    = "ctrl+o"
join_channel  = "n"
leave_channel = "d"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBSettings\fR tab (5) displays all current bindings and their defaults in a
read-only panel.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/meshcore/config.toml
Configuration file.
Created automatically on first save from the Settings tab.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/meshcore/messages.db
bbolt database storing message history and per-contact read positions.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config file uses TOML syntax.
All keys are optional and fall back to built-in defaults.
.PP
.RS
.nf
default_transport = "ble"
default_device    = "AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF"
scan_name_filter  = ""

[profile.home]
transport = "tcp"
device    = "192.168.1.5:3000"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Activate a named profile with \fB\-\-profile home\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the BLE scan picker and choose a device interactively:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui
.RE
.PP
Connect directly to a known BLE address:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui \-\-device AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
.RE
.PP
Connect over serial:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui \-\-transport serial \-\-device /dev/ttyUSB0
.RE
.PP
Connect over TCP:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui \-\-transport tcp \-\-device 192.168.1.100:3000
.RE
.PP
Use a named profile:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui \-\-profile home
.RE
.PP
Capture a debug log:
.PP
.RS
.B meshcore\-tui \-\-debug 2>/tmp/mc.log
.RE
.SH "DELIVERY CONFIRMATION"
Outbound direct messages cycle through the following status indicators:
.TP
.B \&...
BLE write in progress.
.TP
.B \(bu sent
The device accepted the message and has a cached path to the recipient.
.TP
.B \(bu sent (no path)
The device sent the message as a blind mesh flood; no cached route to recipient.
Normal for first contact or after a path has gone stale.
.TP
.B \(ck 42ms
The remote node confirmed receipt (MeshCore \fIPushSendConfirmed\fR event).
Round-trip time is shown.
.TP
.B ? no ack
No confirmation arrived within 30 seconds.
Press \fBr\fR (with input blurred) to retry, up to 3 times.
.PP
After 3 retries with no acknowledgement the app triggers an automatic
\fBflood fallback\fR: \fISendPathDiscoveryReq\fR is sent concurrently with a
final send attempt, forcing the mesh firmware to discover a fresh route to
the peer.
This handles the common mobile-mesh scenario where either party has moved
and the cached path is stale.
No user action is required.
.PP
Channel messages are broadcast.
They show \fB\(ck sent (broadcast)\fR once the device confirms receipt.
There is no per-message remote ack or retry for channel messages.
.SH "BLUETOOTH NOTES"
On Linux, BlueZ must be running (\fBsystemctl status bluetooth\fR).
Install the development headers if building from source:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo apt install bluez libbluetooth-dev
.RE
.PP
On first connection, pair the node:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bluetoothctl
pair AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
trust AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
.fi
.RE
